# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASTROLABE — this project's own edge.
#
# Run as its own compose project (`deploy/edge/compose.yaml`), on this
# project's own Docker network, publishing on the host's LOOPBACK only.
#
# ── Why this file exists at all ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Until now, this site's hostname was served by ANOTHER project's Caddy
# container — one on the same box, owned by a different repository. That edge
# reached this stack's containers by SERVICE NAME, which only resolves from
# inside a Docker network, so the shared edge had to be hand-attached to this
# project's network. `docker compose up` rebuilds a container from its own
# compose definition, so every recreate of that edge dropped the attachment.
#
# That was not theoretical. On 2026-10-21 a traffic flip in the other project
# recreated the shared edge, the attachment was dropped, and this site
# answered 502 for every request while the application container reported
# itself healthy. The symptom read as an outage in THIS project, and nothing
# about this project happened at that moment.
#
# So the edge moves here. The three reasons, in the order they mattered:
#
#   1. BLAST RADIUS. One edge, one process, one config file, three products.
#      A Caddyfile bug, a certificate expiry, a bad upgrade or a traffic flip
#      in ANY project takes down ALL of them.
#   2. TRUST BOUNDARY. A shared edge can reach another project's containers
#      by name, so a compromise or a misconfiguration on that side is a
#      capability against this side.
#   3. THIS FILE IS REVIEWED. The old arrangement put this site's routing in
#      an untracked, host-local file on someone else's box — invisible to
#      review, invisible to `git diff`, and impossible to reason about from
#      the repository. Everything below is in the repository instead.
#
# ── What did NOT change, and why that matters ────────────────────────────────
#
# The site block below is BEHAVIOURALLY IDENTICAL to the block the shared edge
# served: same upstream, same headers, same flush behaviour. A split that
# quietly changes a public site is a release, and this is meant to be an
# invisible change of ownership.
#
# The alternatives that were considered and rejected:
#
#   · KEEP THE SHARED EDGE. It is the status quo, and its measured cost is the
#     outage above. A mitigation that preserves the coupling (reattaching the
#     network on a timer) was tried and is the wrong shape: it keeps the
#     capability alive rather than removing it.
#   · ONE FRONT DOOR THAT TERMINATES TLS. It would move the certificate out of
#     this repository and put a request-inspecting component in front of
#     everything. The front door that IS in front of this edge is an L4 SNI
#     router: it forwards the connection, and this file is still what
#     terminates TLS and answers the request.
#
# ── The certificate ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This edge issues and renews its OWN Let's Encrypt certificate. The ACME
# HTTP-01 challenge is served by this Caddy, over this edge's own :80, and it
# arrives here because the shared front door forwards the plaintext request
# for this hostname to this edge. The front door does not answer the
# challenge, and it holds no certificate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
	# ACME contact address for expiry notices.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL:admin@example.com}
	# The admin API stays internal to the container; never exposed. It also
	# means there is no reload endpoint, so a change to this file needs a
	# container recreate — not a reload.
	admin off
}

# ---- snippets: define before use ------------------------------------------------

# The security headers the shared edge applied to this site. They are
# repeated here rather than shared, because a snippet is global only within
# one Caddy process: each edge carries its own copy, and each copy is
# reviewed with the product it belongs to.
#
# `includeSubDomains; preload` is inherited from the shared edge's setting
# and is kept EXACTLY as it was. Changing a site's HSTS scope during an
# ownership change is a policy decision, not a refactor, and quietly widening
# or narrowing it would be a change nobody asked for.
(secure_defaults) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# This process is a reverse proxy, never a browser-reachable document.
		-Server
	}
	encode zstd gzip
}

# The real domain. DNS for this name is DNS-only (grey cloud), which is what
# lets Caddy issue its own certificate over HTTP-01 — under a proxied record
# the challenge never reaches the origin.
#
# `astrolabe.svalley.tech` is the only name this edge serves, and it is also
# the only name in `nginx/edge.d/astrolabe.sni.map` — a test compares that
# file against this one, so the front door and this edge cannot disagree
# about what this product serves.
astrolabe.svalley.tech {
	import secure_defaults

	reverse_proxy astrolabe:3000 {
		# The client address, and NOT whatever the caller sent. The
		# application reads the RIGHT-most X-Forwarded-For entry as the
		# client, so a caller that prepends an entry gets a fresh rate-limit
		# bucket per request — which is the exact bypass the limiter exists
		# to prevent. Setting the header outright discards anything the
		# client sent.
		#
		# `{remote_host}` is the real client address because the shared
		# front door forwards the TCP stream without rewriting the source
		# address, and this edge is the peer the application sees.
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		# The live feed is a stream; buffering it makes the index look dead.
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
